/** \addtogroup area_backtrack */
/** \defgroup theory_backtrack Theory of backtracking
 \ingroup area_backtrack

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/backtrack` realize the backtrack of
 chapter 16 of the book, i.e., the enumeration of the feasible solutions of a
 problem by a visit in depth of the tree of its partial choices, with its
 exercises (the subsets, the permutations, the \f$ n \f$ queens, the tour of
 the knight, the Sudoku and the anagrams), and beyond the book two families
 of search that rest on the same scheme: the exact cover of Knuth, on dancing
 links and on sparse sets, and the satisfiability of a formula in
 conjunctive normal form, by DPLL and by the learning of clauses. This page
 collects what the headers take for granted: the definitions and the
 notation (see \ref theory_backtrack_notation), the scheme of the
 enumeration with the arguments of its correctness and of its cost (see
 \ref theory_backtrack_scheme), and the properties on which the puzzles, the
 exact cover and the satisfiability rest (from \ref theory_backtrack_puzzles
 to \ref theory_backtrack_sat). The pseudocode of each algorithm is instead
 on the page of its header, in the comment of its function, together with
 the section of the book, or the paper, that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_backtrack_notation Definitions and notation

 A problem of enumeration asks for all the <em>feasible solutions</em> of an
 instance, or for one of them, or for their number; a solution is a vector
 \f$ S[ 0 .. n - 1 ] \f$ whose item \f$ S[ i ] \f$ is taken from a set of
 <em>choices</em> \f$ C( S , i ) \f$ that depends on the items before it
 (section 16.2 of the book). The vectors \f$ S[ 0 .. i - 1 ] \f$ are the
 <em>partial solutions</em>, and they form a tree, the <em>tree of the
 choices</em>, whose root is the empty vector and whose node
 \f$ S[ 0 .. i - 1 ] \f$ has a child \f$ S[ 0 .. i ] \f$ for each choice of
 \f$ C( S , i ) \f$; the feasible solutions are some of its nodes, usually
 leaves. To <em>prune</em> is to leave a subtree unvisited because a test
 on its root proves that it holds no feasible solution.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ S[ 0 .. n - 1 ] \f$, \f$ i \f$</td>
     <td>the vector of a solution and the place being chosen, i.e., the
         depth in the tree of the choices</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ C( S , i ) \f$</td>
     <td>the choices allowed in the place \f$ i \f$ by
         \f$ S[ 0 .. i - 1 ] \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ f( S ) \f$, <tt>visit( S )</tt></td>
     <td>the processing of a solution, which returns true to stop the
         enumeration</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ Q[ r ] \f$</td>
     <td>the column of the queen of the row \f$ r \f$ of the board</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathit{len}( i ) \f$, \f$ \mathrm{size}( x ) \f$</td>
     <td>the options of the item of an exact cover still compatible with
         those chosen</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \ell \f$, \f$ \lnot \ell \f$, \f$ \pm ( v + 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>a literal, its negation, and the code of the literal of the
         variable \f$ v \f$ in a clause of the library, positive or
         negative</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_backtrack_scheme The scheme of the enumeration

 The backtrack of section 16.2 is the recursive visit of the tree of the
 choices: at the node \f$ S[ 0 .. i - 1 ] \f$ it tries each choice
 \f$ c \in C( S , i ) \f$ in turn, sets \f$ S[ i ] \gets c \f$, processes
 \f$ S \f$ if it is a feasible solution, and goes on from
 \f$ S[ 0 .. i ] \f$ unless a test prunes it; when the choices of the place
 \f$ i \f$ are over, it goes back to the place \f$ i - 1 \f$, which is what
 gives the method its name. The only state is \f$ S \f$ itself, updated in
 place and restored on the way back, so that the space is that of the
 deepest path, \f$ O( n ) \f$ items, while the tree may have exponentially
 many nodes.

 <em>Lemma 1 (the enumeration).</em> If every feasible solution is a node of
 the tree of the choices and each pruned subtree holds none, the backtrack
 processes each feasible solution exactly once, in the lexicographic order
 of the choices when \f$ C( S , i ) \f$ is scanned in increasing order.

 Indeed, two nodes of the tree are distinct vectors, a visit in depth meets
 each node of the subtrees it enters once, and the subtrees it skips hold no
 solution by hypothesis. The order follows since the visit in depth of an
 ordered tree meets its nodes in the lexicographic order of their paths
 from the root. The cost is the number of nodes visited times the cost of
 a node, i.e., of building \f$ C( S , i ) \f$ and of the tests: for the
 \f$ k \f$-subsets of `subsets()` in `Enumeration.h`, where a place is in or
 out, the tree has fewer than \f$ 2^{n+1} \f$ nodes, and the pruning of the
 nodes with \f$ k \f$ items in, or with too few items left to reach
 \f$ k \f$, leaves only the nodes on a path to one of the
 \f$ \binom{n}{k} \f$ solutions, i.e., at most
 \f$ ( n + 1 ) \binom{n}{k} \f$; for
 the permutations of `permutations()` the nodes of the depth \f$ i \f$ are
 \f$ n! / ( n - i )! \f$, and in all
 \f[
   \sum_{i=0}^{n} \frac{n!}{( n - i )!} = n! \sum_{j=0}^{n} \frac{1}{j!}
   < e \, n! , \tag{1}
 \f]
 so that the work per permutation is \f$ O( n ) \f$, the cost of the scan of
 the items not used at each node. The derangements are the permutations
 pruned at the place \f$ i \f$ by the item \f$ i \f$, and the subsets of
 `subsetSum()`, whose items are positive, are pruned as soon as their sum
 exceeds \f$ k \f$, since no item added later can lower it.

 The recursion is a convenience, not a need: the iterative backtrack of
 `nQueensIterative()` keeps the place \f$ k \f$ and the choice
 \f$ V[ k ] \f$ of each place, advances \f$ V[ k ] \f$ to its next feasible
 value, and moves \f$ k \f$ forward when one is found and backward when the
 values are over, i.e., the same visit with the stack of the calls replaced
 by the vector \f$ V \f$ itself.

 \section theory_backtrack_puzzles Queens, tours of the knight and Sudoku

 The \f$ n \f$ queens are placed one per row, since two queens on a row
 attack each other; a solution is then the vector \f$ Q \f$ of their
 columns, a permutation, and the tree has at most \f$ e \, n! \f$ nodes by
 (1), against the \f$ \binom{n^2}{n} \f$ placements of \f$ n \f$ queens on
 the board. Two queens \f$ ( r , Q[ r ] ) \f$ and \f$ ( r' , Q[ r' ] ) \f$
 share a diagonal if and only if \f$ | Q[ r ] - Q[ r' ] | = | r - r' | \f$,
 which is the test of `admissible()` of `NQueens.h`, \f$ O( n^2 ) \f$ on a
 whole placement. `nQueens()` instead keeps three sets of the columns
 attacked on the current row, by the columns taken and by the two families
 of diagonals; moving to the next row shifts the sets of the diagonals by one
 column, one to the left and one to the right, and the free columns of a row
 are the complement of their union. As masks of \f$ n \le 32 \f$ bits these
 are a few operations on a word, and the next free column is its lowest 1
 bit, so that each node of the tree costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$.

 The tour of the knight of `KnightTour.h` is a Hamiltonian path in the graph
 of the moves of the knight, whose nodes are the \f$ n^2 \f$ squares and
 have degree at most 8; the backtrack extends the path by a move to a square
 not visited yet, and goes back from the squares with no such move. In the
 order of the book the tree may be explored for a very long time before a
 tour is found. The rule of Warnsdorff tries first the moves to the squares
 with the fewest onward moves, i.e., it visits first the squares that would
 otherwise become dead ends; it is a heuristic, with no bound on the
 backtracks it saves, but on the boards of practical size it finds a tour
 almost without going back, i.e., in time about linear in \f$ n^2 \f$.

 The Sudoku of `Sudoku.h` fills the \f$ n^4 \f$ squares of a grid of side
 \f$ N = n^2 \f$ in the order of the rows, each with a number that is not on
 its row, its column or its block (`check()`, \f$ O( N ) \f$), and the
 given squares are skipped. The tree has depth \f$ n^4 \f$ and at most
 \f$ N \f$ children per node; the cost is therefore a constant for the
 classic \f$ n = 3 \f$, a large one, and grows superpolynomially with
 \f$ n \f$, as the problem is NP-complete on grids of side \f$ n^2 \f$. The
 anagrams of `Anagrams.h` are the permutations of the multiset of the
 characters that split into words: choosing each distinct character once
 per place gives each permutation of the multiset once, and a test of the
 prefixes of the words prunes a branch as soon as its current word cannot be
 completed.

 \section theory_backtrack_cover Exact cover

 Given a set of <em>items</em>, some primary and some secondary, and a
 family of <em>options</em>, each a set of items, an <em>exact cover</em> is
 a set of options that contains each primary item exactly once and each
 secondary item at most once. Many puzzles are exact covers: the queens with
 the rows and the columns as primary items and the diagonals as secondary
 ones, the Sudoku with the 324 primary items (a square, a row with a
 number, a column with a number, a block with a number) and the options of
 the \f$ 9^3 \f$ numbers in the squares. The search (Knuth's Algorithm X)
 is a backtrack whose place is a primary item not covered yet and whose
 choices are the options that contain it and none of the items already
 covered: choosing an option covers its items, and removes from the
 problem every option that meets one of them.

 <em>Lemma 2 (the dancing links).</em> On a circular doubly linked list
 with the links \f$ L \f$ and \f$ R \f$, the removal of a node \f$ x \f$ by
 \f[
   R[ L[ x ] ] \gets R[ x ] , \quad L[ R[ x ] ] \gets L[ x ] \tag{2}
 \f]
 leaves \f$ L[ x ] \f$ and \f$ R[ x ] \f$ as they were; hence the
 assignments \f$ R[ L[ x ] ] \gets x \f$ and \f$ L[ R[ x ] ] \gets x \f$ put it
 back, provided that the removals done after that of \f$ x \f$ have been
 undone, in the reverse order, before.

 Indeed, after the later removals are undone the neighbors of \f$ x \f$
 are again \f$ L[ x ] \f$ and \f$ R[ x ] \f$, which (2) did not touch. In
 `ExactCover` of `DancingLinks.h` the items are a list and the nodes of each
 item, one per option that contains it, a vertical list; covering an item
 takes it off its list and the other nodes of each of its options off their
 vertical lists, and uncovering undoes the same steps in the reverse order,
 so that the links after the search are those before it (an audit of the
 class). Each removal and each reinsertion costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$, and the
 work of a node of the tree is the number of links updated, the measure of
 `updates()`. The search branches on the primary item with the fewest
 options left (the MRV heuristic): an item with none ends the branch at
 once, and an item with one forces its option without branching, which
 keeps the tree small.

 `FastExactCover` of `FastExactCover.h` solves the exact cover with colors
 (Algorithm C), where an option may give a color to a secondary item and
 several options may share the item if they give it the same color. The
 options of each item are a <em>sparse set</em> of Briggs and Torczon, a
 segment of an array whose first \f$ \mathrm{size}( x ) \f$ entries are the
 active options of \f$ x \f$, each node knowing its place: an option leaves
 the set by a swap with the last active entry and a decrement of the size,
 in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$. The removed entries stay in the segment, after the
 active ones; hence restoring a level needs no inverse swap, only the sizes
 of that level written back, and the sets are again those of the level, in
 another order. This costs \f$ O( a ) \f$ per level, \f$ a \f$ the active
 items, against an update per link of the dancing links, and the memory is
 read by consecutive indices. In both classes the worst case stays
 exponential, the exact cover being NP-complete.

 \section theory_backtrack_sat Satisfiability

 A formula in conjunctive normal form is a set of <em>clauses</em>, each a
 disjunction of <em>literals</em>, i.e., of variables and of negated
 variables; it is satisfiable if some assignment of truth values makes every
 clause true (a model). A clause of a partial assignment is
 <em>satisfied</em> if one of its literals is true, <em>conflicting</em> if
 all are false, and <em>unit</em> if all are false but one, which is free
 and then must be true in every model that extends the assignment.

 <em>Lemma 3 (the unit propagation).</em> Making true the free literal of a
 unit clause, until no clause is unit or one is conflicting, loses no model
 of the formula that extends the assignment; a conflicting clause proves
 that there is none.

 The procedure of Davis, Putnam, Logemann and Loveland (`dpll()` of
 `SatSolvers.h`) alternates this propagation with a branch on a free
 literal, both values tried, and goes back on a conflict; by Lemma 1 and
 Lemma 3 it is complete, and its tree has at most \f$ 2^n \f$ leaves for
 \f$ n \f$ variables. Branching on a literal of a shortest open clause makes
 the propagation of the branch likely to produce more units.

 The learning of clauses of `Cdcl` keeps the literals assigned in a
 <em>trail</em>, each with its <em>level</em> (the number of decisions before
 it) and its <em>reason</em> (the clause that forced it, none for a
 decision). A conflict is analyzed by resolution: the conflicting clause is
 resolved with the reasons of its literals of the current level, from the
 last assigned backwards, until a single literal of that level is left, the
 <em>first unique implication point</em>. The clause \f$ C \f$ so obtained
 is implied by the formula, since resolution preserves the models; it is
 false under the trail, and after the levels above the second largest level
 of its literals are cancelled it is unit, so that it forces the negation of
 the implication point there (the jump back, possibly over several levels).
 The same assignment that led to the conflict can then never occur again,
 and the search is complete, since the number of clauses that can be
 learned is finite. Each clause <em>watches</em> two of its literals, with
 the invariant that a clause whose watched literals are both false is
 conflicting or satisfied; a literal that becomes false thus visits only
 the clauses that watch it, and the cancelling of levels needs no update
 of the watches. The decisions take the free variable of largest activity,
 increased at each conflict for the variables met in the analysis and
 decayed by raising the increment (VSIDS), with the value it had last (the
 phase saving); the restarts cancel all the levels after a number of
 conflicts that follows the sequence of Luby, Sinclair and Zuckerman,
 \f$ 1 , 1 , 2 , 1 , 1 , 2 , 4 , \ldots \f$ times 100, which keeps the learned
 clauses and the activities and, for a search whose length is unknown, is
 within a logarithmic factor of the best strategy of restarts.

